relatedPosts.content:
@John Hart: I disagree.  Gasoline has a higher energy density that will never be surpassed.  It's also more stable and has simpler logistics.  Plus, gas cars hold their value far better than EVs.

EVs are good as little urban commuters, but we should really be turning to mass transit and sharing for that use case.  So every sale of an EV as a private personal car represents a failure of policy.

All the hard use cases will be filled by gas cars, and all the easy use cases should be replaced with mass transit and far fewer vehicles, not a new fleet of EVs.  In 30 years, if we are lucky, there will be fewer personal EVs than gas engines.  Because there will be so many fewer total cars.

@Z I view this as “a Kodak Moment”. An ossified organization that refused to undercut its long standing revenue stream based on old technology. Kodak continued to protect its position until it was too depleted to change and ended up being eaten up by its digital imaging competitors. A good friend of mine was recruited  to try to help them go digital.  It was like trying to ask a doctor to resuscitate a person who has been in an unwitnessed cardiac arrest and expecting the outcome to be perfect, i.e. impossible. I remember him talking about them having to sell off forests, paper mills, chemical plants and laying off 10’s of thousands of employees in an effort to scrape together enough capital to fund their digital conversion. By then it was too late. I am a physician and can honestly say that it has been close to 20 years since I last looked at an x-ray of an image captured on Kodak film.  Even longer for an image from a film camera. 

I haven’t driven a ICE car since 2012.

Trump and the DOE are helping the big three speed to their “Kodak Moment”.

@Liz Lemon

I read here just yesterday that, in the decade following the Beijing “airpocalypse”,

“China grew by roughly 50 million people - more than the entire population of Canada. But air pollution didn't scale up as the population grew; it declined by half. Leaders and the public in China decided that the smog was unacceptable. The authorities put into effect new regulations and requirements on coal-fired power plants and heavy industry. The government devoted new resources to monitoring and enforcement. Many polluting factories and power plants adopted cleaner technologies…”

@JRS
Also a MachE owner here. The car is a pure joy to drive. Quick and smooth acceleration. None of the hesitations one would experience in a gasoline powered vehicle. You hear only wind and road noise. No engine noise and vibration. Other than saving on energy cost, I don’t have to pay for oil changes and other maintenance one has to do on internal combustion engines. 

Range issue is limited to long distance driving. That is not a vehicle problem. It is an infrastructure problem. Charging stations are few and not conveniently located. My gasoline cars won’t go far also if there is no gas stations along highways.  I think that’s where the US loses out on EV. This is a market inhibitor for the US EV market.

@MarkS

We live in the cloudiest city in America and our rooftop solar powers our Kia EV, which we bought used (2,000 miles) at half the price of new. It is fabulous.

We had a Tesla—loved the technology, hated the car—was totaled in a fender-bender (it is virtually impossible to get them repaired at a sane price).

@Opinionated Possum

Also, and I don't know how to say this, but China is a collectivist culture that values education and excellence. They were the most advanced civilization on the planet for centuries, and they want that back. They have a much greater work ethic, too. 

Meanwhile, Americans have become soft, begging for cushy union jobs and SSDI and fentanyl.

@Tall Trees and Trains. It's the Kodak Syndrome.  Old guys in charge and can't give up their old, but dying cash cow.

And to think most of the tech was developed by NASA at taxpayers expense. Boy are we getting shafted.

Jus sayin.

@Brian

If you buy a Chinese EV in Canada wouldn't you have to pay 100% duty to bring it across the border?

Granted, your USD is worth considerably more in Canada due to the exchange rate.

@Annie

"chargers move along a ceiling trolley, making it easy for people to charge their cars in any spot while they park."

May be a great idea for condos with underground parking.

